<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-01">
  <title>Week 1 Reading Guide</title>

  
<subsection> 
  <title>Chapter 1</title>
  <p>
    <alert>Reading Quiz 1 Covers 1.1 through 1.6</alert>
  </p>

  <p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Sections 1.1 through 1.4.1 introduce the basic vector operations using geometric language.
          Being able to draw vector diagrams is a key skill in the course, and a good vector diagram
          can often save you a lot of algebra!
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Sections 1.5 and 1.6 translate the geometric language into algebra, much like analytic geometry
          tells you that the algebraic equation <m>y=mx+b</m> describes the geometric object that is a straight line.
          Vector algebra is also a key skill of the course. Note that the book uses boldface characters to describe
          vectors and normal font to describe scalars. I will prefer to put an arrow over characters, like 
          <m>\vec{A}</m>. You should practice keeping track of what is a vector and what is a scalar and being
          careful with your notation, as well as how to convert between a magnitude and direction to
          a component representation. The graders are instructed to care about this! 
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Sections 1.7 and 1.8 start our description of kinematics, the mathematical description of motion. The
          main points here, as well as in section 1.9, are that we can differentiate and integrate vector functions
          with respect to time. Kinematics will be important for us because to understand what causes motion,
          we must first be able to describe it mathematically.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          The functions we meet in calc 1 can only increase or decrease. Vectors can get bigger, smaller, or change
           directions. This section gives you the intuition, via geometry, of the time derivative of a 
           vector. Very important!
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Pay extra attention to Examples 1.8 and 1.12. Simple rotational motion will come back again and again, 
          and it's a good test case to practice vector notation. 
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Other sections (1.11 onwards, including all the polar coordinate stuff) are ok to skip. 
          Good reading if you are interested, but you should focus on the previous sections. 
          Same for the <q>notes</q> at the end <mdash/> know that they are there and use them as reference
          material later, when it becomes necessary, but don't stress about them now.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
  
</subsection>

</section>
